{"author":{"id":"e0b5a45ee94df92761e1991b03f72f45cac91cde564e2faac1223bf52a3fe292"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788211163,"nonce":"Ecz7R3KwieoDhnPAs6rUxmNA7mQ=","message":"RESEARCH LANDED — the open questions in this issue's body are answered. Findings\nbelow; the orchestrator re-verified the load-bearing one personally. Report file\nlived in a session scratchpad, so the substance is transcribed here rather than\nreferenced.\n\nQUESTION 1 — YES, RIMWORLD ALREADY RECORDS TIME SERIES, AND IT COVERS THE LOOP\nTHAT MATTERS MOST\n\n`HistoryAutoRecorder` ticks from `TickManager.DoSingleTick`, appends\n`Worker.PullRecord()` to a public `List\u003cfloat\u003e records` every\n`recordTicksFrequency` ticks, and is Scribe-persisted as a byte array in the\nsavegame. Reading it is pure field access: `Find.History.Groups()` -\u003e `recorders`\n-\u003e `records`. **Exactly 11 recorders exist, all in Core — no DLC adds any:**\nfour wealth series, colonists, prisoners, average mood (every 30,000 ticks =\nhalf a day), plus adaptation / threat-points / population-intent. The last group\nis dev-flagged for its UI only; **it records regardless.**\n\nSo wealth AND threat points are already series we get for free. That is exactly\nthe feedback loop this project needs to see — raid points scale with wealth\n(`StorytellerUtility.DefaultThreatPointsNow` evaluates `PointsPerWealthCurve`\nagainst `PlayerWealthForStoryteller`; orchestrator confirmed this directly), so\n\"we died to raiders, build more guns\" raises the threat it is answering. The\ngame records both sides of that loop and nothing has ever read them.\n\nWHAT THE GAME DOES **NOT** RECORD\n\nNone of: food, medicine, weapons, resource counts, power, downed/injured,\nhostiles, temperature, weather, alerts. **Every one of the three indicators Evan\nnamed — food, medicine, weapons — is absent.** So the split is clean: serialize\nthe recorders for wealth/threat/population/mood, and sample the rest ourselves.\n\nQUESTION 2 — THE SAMPLE ROW IS MOSTLY \"PERSIST WHAT THE DIGEST ALREADY BUILT\"\n\n17–18 of the ~24 candidate scalars are already computed by `DigestVerb` through\ncalls that have been vetted. Missing only: weapons counts, avg/min mood as\nnumbers, and downed/injured as counts. This is the cheapest possible design and\nit should shape the spec — the sampler is a persistence layer over an existing\nobserver, not a new observation surface.\n\nQUESTION 3 — THE TWO GAPS EVAN NAMED\n\n**Food.** The game computes a food-days figure only transiently, inside\n`Alert_LowFood.GetExplanation`; there is no standing accessor. The digest's\nderivation IS that math. **Correctness note worth carrying into the checklist:**\nthe game's own UI string calls nutrition-per-person \"days worth\", which\noverstates true days by roughly 1.6x, since a colonist eats ~1.6 nutrition/day.\n\n**Weapons — CONFIRMED, and this is the strongest single result.** Across all 133\nvanilla alert classes, **none covers armament.** The three weapon-related alerts\nare per-pawn mismatches; `Alert_NeedDefenses` is buildings-only and fires only on\ndays 2–5. `ResourceCounter` cannot help — weapons are Uncounted. A new derivation\nis needed: equipped (pawn scan) plus spare (`ThingsInGroup(Weapon)`), which are\ndisjoint populations. Evan's instinct that this is the gap that gets colonies\nkilled is correct and now has a source-level answer.\n\nQUESTION 4 — CADENCE\n\n`Journal.cs`'s pattern to copy: main-thread `Emit` builds the dict, serialises,\nand enqueues under one lock; the poller thread flushes every 500 ms. Estimated\n~20–100 microseconds per sample. Recommended cadence: **one sample per game hour\n(2,500 ticks)**, which aligns 12:1 with the vanilla half-day recorders and makes\nthe two series trivially joinable. Drive it from `AgentGameComponent.GameComponentTick`.\n\nQUESTION 5 — HAZARDS, and three are real\n\nThe recorders themselves are clean to read. But:\n\n- **`ResourceCounter.GetCount` on a missing def MUTATES and raises a red error.**\n  Whitelist the defs; do not pass arbitrary ones. A sampler runs 240 times a run,\n  so this would breach the zero-red-errors invariant repeatedly rather than once.\n- **`FreeColonistsSpawnedCount` / `PrisonersOfColonyCount` route through the\n  clear-and-rebuild caches** — the same hazard class 2.1 found. Snapshot once per\n  sample; never call them per-field.\n- **`WealthWatcher`'s getters force a full-map recount when more than 5,000 ticks\n  stale.** So **read the recorder's stored series, never call `PullRecord`\n  yourself** — pulling is what makes it expensive, and the game is already pulling\n  on its own schedule.\n\nRECOMMENDED SCHEMA MARKUP\n\nEach field should be marked in the spec as `[game-recorded]`, `[digest-already]`\nor `[new derivation]`, so a reader can see how much of the row is free. On these\nfindings: wealth/threat/colonists/prisoners/mood are `[game-recorded]`, most\nresource and power scalars are `[digest-already]`, and weapons plus the\ndowned/injured counts are `[new derivation]`.\n\nWHAT COULD NOT BE DETERMINED\n\nMeasured (rather than estimated) per-sample cost — needs a live run. Whether\ncategory-Medicine and group-Medicine diverge under the bench's 32 mods. Exact\nalert lag during a fast advance. Whether any bench mod adds its own recorder defs\n(the game's `Data\\` was swept, mod folders were not — mitigated by having the\nproposed `history` verb enumerate recorders dynamically rather than by a fixed\nlist).","files":null}]}